  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "do_acl_entry",
    "file": "opensc/src/pkcs15init/profile.c",
    "description": "The root cause is unsafe ACL pointer encoding in profile parsing: ACL entries parsed from fuzzed profile text can assign raw integer values (e.g., 0x9000) into sc_file_t->acl[] instead of valid heap nodes or the three reserved sentinels (1/2/3). Later, sc_profile_free()->free_file_list()->sc_file_free() calls sc_file_clear_acl_entries(), which treats any non-NULL/non-1/2/3 value as a linked-list head and dereferences e->next, causing wild read/SEGV. Crash is observed in sc_file_clear_acl_entries, but missing validation/sanitization occurs when parsing ACL config in do_acl_entry.",
    "conditions": "Trigger by providing a malformed pkcs15init profile string (via fuzz_pkcs15init input) that creates file definitions with crafted ACL/access rules so file->acl[op] becomes an invalid non-sentinel pointer value. During cleanup path (e.g., sc_profile_finish failure or normal teardown), sc_profile_free frees file list and sc_file_free iterates ACL ops; sc_file_clear_acl_entries dereferences the attacker-influenced bogus pointer and crashes."
  },
